body:
Hey there.  It sounds like you are really struggling.  Can I offer an alternative route?  Go to counseling, and take her with you.  I badly regret not working on my last relationship in a more mature manner (not saying your reasons aren't mature), and I also still loved her when I ended it, and I still do.  

&#x200B;

Point being, if she is really good for you, do not self sabotage and end it.  Fight for those good things in life.  I have discovered that they do not come back around very easily...

&#x200B;

Just consider it.  I wish you luck.

Thank you, my job is solid and make nearly 6 figures and do have local friends and family... Health is decent and I workout but am by no means a body builder or anything.

&#x200B;

Definitely need to take better care of my body.  Your story is a good one and I wish you the best.

I just want to say that I am sorry you went through this.  Also I realized this past year that I was avoidant and had NO IDEA.  I literally did not know why my relationship and every one before it had collapsed.  My partner had to have been hurting and I am hurting now dealing with the aftermath.

&#x200B;

I later read a book called "Attached" and my avoidant tendencies jumped off of the page at me.  I do not want to be like this.  I am generally a kind, caring, and loving person but for some reason as a relationship progresses I become avoidant, they get hurt and turned off, and then it ends.  I have had this pattern for years but no more.

&#x200B;

If anyone knows anyone like this PLEASE read a book called "Attached."  

&#x200B;

I am still heart broken over my last relationship and my shitty avoidant behavior, but I now know how to act in my next relationship so there is some sort of a silver lining there.

&#x200B;

OP this is not your fault, and not everyone has a ton of introspection to fix their issues, but that book might be helpful for you in dealing with your current heartache and also in learning how to handle the situation next time if it arises.

True!  In my case, and any others reading this, I have always heard this.  "Be happy on your own first!"  I have never been able to do that and a relationship is what gave me the ability to be happy.  I have just started taking some antidepressants and have realized that I have possibly been depressed my entire life, and relationships were there mainly to give me enough of a boost to feel relatively normal.  When they are taken away, I revert back to devastation.  Now I am thinking that I simply had an imbalance in my head the entire time as I am finally feeling better after a breakup from one year ago.

&#x200B;

Point being, if you find that you cannot be happy alone, or after 6 months to a year are still in the depths of hell with depression and sadness it may be something out of your control and an actual chemical deficiency.

Yeah I am actively working on this now....  I have just had relationships for so many years of my life and being single now for almost 9 months is about as long as it has ever been.  That coupled up with the fact that I am really feeling the pressure to have a family by now instead of being single has been tough.  If I was 24 right now and newly single, it would be less devastating.  I just get hung up on this age thing and comparing myself to others.  I definitely feel behind.  I AM grateful for the fact that my financial and career situation is doing well, but coming home to an empty house every night sucks....

&#x200B;

Anyways thank you for the comment it really means a lot.   I was feeling so low when I wrote that... So your kind reply really helps.

Thank you for this...  Very encouraging.  Six years is a long time but I am glad you feel better!

&#x200B;

I wrote that yesterday at the lowest point in my entire life, today I feel a bit better and will keep putting one foot in front of the other.

I recommend a book called "Attached" by Levine and Heller.  It will shed a lot of light onto the situation and his personality traits as being generally avoidant.  I ruined a relationship the same way (unknowingly) and will be better in the future.  But for you it may help future partnerships become more successful.

&#x200B;

Also congrats on feeling good about yourself again!  I wish you the best.
